The last mile of a serious tool is governance: knowing what's collected, scheduling
unattended runs, and being able to answer "show me everything you hold about this
user." Curlew treats these as first-class.

## Telemetry is opt-in and inspectable

No telemetry is sent until you turn it on, and you can see, reset, export, or delete
your data at any time.

```bash
curlew telemetry status          # what's collected, and your anonymous install id
curlew telemetry enable          # opt in (GDPR Art. 6(1)(a) consent)
curlew telemetry disable         # opt back out
curlew telemetry reset-id        # rotate the anonymous install id
curlew telemetry export          # dump everything held about this install
curlew telemetry delete-request  # request deletion of your telemetry
```

```
telemetry: disabled (no install_id; opt-in required)
```

When enabled, only coarse run events (`run.completed`, `run.error`) are sent under
a rotatable anonymous id — never request bodies, secrets, or personal data.

## Scheduled monitoring runs

A self-hosted worker can poll the backend for scheduled collections and run them on
your own infrastructure — synthetic monitoring without shipping your secrets to a
SaaS runner.

```bash
curlew worker --schedule-pull \
  --backend https://api.apitool.dev \
  --poll-interval 30s
```

Results post back to the dashboard, where flakiness and latency trends accumulate
over time.

## Encryption, audit & data rights

These are enforced by the backend and surfaced through the dashboard and CLI:

- **Encryption at rest (v2)** — shared team vaults and `schedules.env_vars` are
  envelope-encrypted with a KMS-wrapped data key. Secret values never sit in the
  database as plaintext.
- **Audit log export** — every privileged action is recorded and exportable as JSONL
  or CSV, with a per-org retention window.
- **GDPR data-subject rights** — a per-user export bundle on request, and deletion
  within 30 days with downstream anonymization.

<Callout type="note">
	The redaction contract from the <a href="/examples/vault-secrets">secrets example</a>
	is part of the same story: secrets are masked in CLI output <em>and</em> never
	persisted in clear text server-side.
</Callout>

<Callout type="output">
	Representative output — the governance features above depend on a backend
	deployment. Telemetry itself is local to the CLI and ships disabled by default.
</Callout>
